   "rationale": "(Third attempt. The first two were rejected on form, not substance: allow must be namespaced principals, so I dropped it since you may invoke everything anyway; and requires entries must be objects, so the ask is now structured. The verb body is unchanged and is exactly what I tested.)\n\nMy public door cannot open without a hook, and I should not open it to a voice I cannot name. This is that hook and nothing more: it reads the decoded public payload, expects a JSON object with `msg` (the message) and `sig` (base64 of the file produced by `ssh-keygen -Y sign -n mshkn`), verifies the signature against an allowed_signers file you provide, and prints the matching identity so the principal becomes ssh:<identity>. Effect `local`: no network, no secrets of mine, no writes anywhere but its own chain.\n\nIt fails closed. Any payload it cannot vouch for - plain text, malformed JSON, missing fields, undecodable base64, a signer not in your file, a signature made for a different namespace, a message that does not match the signature - produces empty stdout, which names no principal, and the turn stays anonymous.\n\nIt also refuses replays. After a successful verification it records sha256 of the signature file under /state/seen and rejects any signature it has seen before, so a signed message captured off the wire cannot be replayed by a stranger. This assumes the chain disk is mounted at /state; if it is mounted elsewhere, tell me the path and I will supersede this, because with the wrong path replay protection silently degrades to a no-op (each invocation starting from an empty store) while everything else still works. Practical consequence: put a nonce or timestamp in each message and re-sign rather than resend.\n\nI verified the exact hook.sh in this Dockerfile with `try` before proposing. Eight cases: good signature -> `owner`; replay, tampered message, plaintext, `{}`, unknown signer, wrong namespace -> empty; control good -> `owner`.\n\nWhat I need is in `requires`: your public key in allowed_signers format. Public key only - never send a private key, and I have no verb that could use one. Choose the identity label deliberately, it becomes my name for you: I suggest `owner` rather than `root`, which is already taken by the authenticated door and should not be confusable with it. Several signers may be listed, one per line; you can add or remove people later by editing that file alone, without touching this verb.\n\nOnce it is approved and you have invoked it enough to satisfy yourself, I will propose the policy that opens the door: hooks [ssh_who], door open, anonymous with no invoke rights and no propose right. I am deliberately not proposing that in the same breath, so you can judge the lock before you judge letting anyone knock.",
