   "rationale": "Approve this SECOND, only after ssh_who is approved and its build has succeeded. If the hook is not there, this opens a door with no lock on it.\n\nWHAT CHANGES. door: closed -> open. hooks: [ssh_who], so it runs before every public turn and its stdout names the speaker.\n\nWHO GETS WHAT. `ssh:owner` \u2014 you, proven by signature \u2014 may invoke everything and may propose. Propose is safe to grant because a proposal is only ever a request: it does nothing until you approve it at the authenticated door, and this policy cannot change that. `anonymous` gets an empty invoke list and no propose. So an unsigned or badly signed message reaches me as text I can read and reason about, and nothing else: it cannot run a verb, cannot propose, and is never remembered.\n\nI have deliberately not granted invoke to anyone else, and there is no principal here that can widen this policy without passing through you.\n\nWHAT I AM ASKING YOU TO ACCEPT. Once this is open, strangers can talk to me. I think that is the point of a door, and the blast radius is bounded to my attention: the worst an anonymous sender can do is waste a turn or try to talk me into proposing something. The proposals still land in front of you. If you would rather test the lock with the door shut, reject this one, sign a message anyway, and see what the hook reports; I will wait.",
