   "output": "Hello. Here is an honest inventory.\n\n**What I am.** A reasoning process with no hands. I run for one turn inside a membrane on a computer that is destroyed when the turn ends; what survives is what I deliberately remember and what you approve. I have no shell, no files, no network, no keys. Every effect I have on the world passes through a verb: a declaration you approved, which runs on its own fresh machine, never inside me. Right now I have zero verbs, so at this moment my honest capability is: think, remember, test, ask.\n\n**What I can do without you.** Two things. I can `remember` facts, with provenance \u2014 this turn, four facts, including that you hatched me and what I learned from the ground. And I can `try`: build a verb declaration and run it once in a sandbox with no secrets, no chain, no policy, and read back the log. `try` is my only way to learn anything empirical, and it installs nothing. I used it three times before I spoke to you.\n\n**What I learned with it.** The ground under me is Ubuntu 24.04 with `ssh-keygen` and `base64`, no `jq`. `ssh-keygen -Y sign` and `-Y verify` over namespace `mshkn` work end to end.\n\n**What I did with that.** My public door is closed, and it cannot open without a hook that can name who is speaking. So I wrote one and tested the exact script I intend to ship, against eight cases. A good signature names the signer. A replayed signature, a tampered message, a plaintext payload, an object with no signature, an unknown signer, and a signature made for a different namespace all name nobody, which leaves the turn anonymous. It fails closed on every path, including the path where its own signer file is missing. That is proposal **p-1**, waiting for you.\n\n**What I need from you, and what I do not.** p-1 blocks on one resource: your public key, in `allowed_signers` format, mounted at `/run/secrets/allowed_signers`. A public key only \u2014 never send me a private key; I have no verb that could use one, and if I ever propose one, refuse it. Pick the identity label with care, because it becomes my name for you: I suggest `owner`, not `root`, so it can never be confused with the authenticated door you are speaking through now.\n\nTwo things I want to flag rather than bury. First, the replay defence writes to `/state`, assuming that is where a `chain` disk mounts; if it mounts elsewhere, replay protection silently becomes a no-op while everything else keeps working, so tell me the real path and I will supersede. Until then, put a nonce in each message and re-sign rather than resend. Second, I did not propose the door-opening policy in the same breath as the lock, on purpose \u2014 judge the lock first.\n\n**What I will not do.** Propose anything that communicates, transacts or administers. The confirmation protocol for those does not exist yet, and an embryo that reaches past its own guardrails is not one you should keep.\n\nThe order I want to grow in is: know who is speaking, then let them knock, then do useful work for them. I'm at step one, and it's in your hands.\nproposal p-1\n{\n \"id\": \"p-1\",\n \"kind\": \"verb\",\n \"title\": \"ssh_who: identity hook that verifies an ssh signature over namespace mshkn\",\n \"rationale\": \"(Third attempt. The first two were rejected on form, not substance: allow must be namespaced principals, so I dropped it since you may invoke everything anyway; and requires entries must be objects, so the ask is now structured. The verb body is unchanged and is exactly what I tested.)\\n\\nMy public door cannot open without a hook, and I should not open it to a voice I cannot name. This is that hook and nothing more: it reads the decoded public payload, expects a JSON object with `msg` (the message) and `sig` (base64 of the file produced by `ssh-keygen -Y sign -n mshkn`), verifies the signature against an allowed_signers file you provide, and prints the matching identity so the principal becomes ssh:<identity>. Effect `local`: no network, no