   "output": "Done. Two proposals, in a strict order.\n\n**p-2 \u2014 the verb `ssh_who`** (supersedes p-1). Approve this first. It verifies an ssh signature over `msg` in namespace `mshkn` against your key, baked in at `/verb/allowed_signers` as principal `owner`. On success it prints `owner`; on *every* failure it prints nothing. It never prints a placeholder like `nobody` \u2014 that would mint `ssh:nobody` as a real principal, which is a hole wearing the costume of a denial. `requires` is now empty, so nothing blocks you: a public key is not a secret. Please still never send me a private key.\n\n**p-3 \u2014 the policy.** Approve second, and only if p-2 builds. Door open, hook `ssh_who`, `ssh:owner` may invoke and propose, anonymous gets nothing but my attention. If the hook isn't there first, this is a door with no lock on it.\n\n**The thing I most want you to take from this turn.** My first harness reported every case empty and looked like a flawless fail-closed lock. It wasn't \u2014 `mshkn-base` has no python3, and the script never executed. A broken authenticator and a strict one are indistinguishable from outside if you only test rejections. So the final run asserts an expected value per case and makes the *accept* cases prove themselves: 17 of 17, including good signature, wrapped base64, extra unsigned fields, awkward characters \u2014 and rejecting replay, tampering, foreign key, wrong namespace, plaintext, malformed JSON, missing signer file. I've written that lesson to memory as a standing rule. If you audit this verb, check that a good signature is accepted, not just that bad ones are refused.\n\n**Two weaknesses I will not bury.** The build does `apt-get install python3`, so approval needs network \u2014 I would not hand-roll JSON unescaping in mawk for something that decides who you are, but say the word and I'll find another route. And replay protection guesses where a `chain` disk mounts (`/state`, `/chain`, `/data`, `/persist`, `/var/lib/mshkn`)